WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:08.476
This is Chapter 4: AWS Health.

4
00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:13.450
AWS tells each account which of its
instances will be retired, and when.

5
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.104
The API that says so needs a support plan,

6
00:00:16.104 --> 00:00:19.562
a policy you write yourself,
and the right partition.

7
00:00:19.562 --> 00:00:23.439
We'll start from AWS's own
documentation for the Health API,

8
00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:26.993
see what it leaves to you,
then read the kit's collector,

9
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:32.324
its kinds and its partition guard, and walk
two labs, the evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:32.324 --> 00:00:33.900
Two notes before we begin.

11
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.262
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

12
00:00:37.262 --> 00:00:41.150
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.013
Why this chapter?

14
00:00:46.013 --> 00:00:49.152
AWS already tells each
account, in writing,

15
00:00:49.152 --> 00:00:52.201
which instances will be retired and when.

16
00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:55.638
The gap is that nobody reads it
unless something collects it.

17
00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.287
So the goal is concrete.

18
00:00:57.287 --> 00:01:02.386
By the end you can read every open and
upcoming AWS Health event for an account,

19
00:01:02.386 --> 00:01:06.449
with a policy that allows three
read actions and nothing else,

20
00:01:06.449 --> 00:01:10.033
into records that name the
affected resources and say

21
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.538
whether AWS or the kit decided
the event needs action.

22
00:01:13.538 --> 00:01:17.953
It's for developers who maintain the kit,
the account owner who owns this gate,

23
00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:20.840
and whoever owns the
account's support plan.

24
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.165
Next, which partition.

25
00:01:26.165 --> 00:01:29.565
AWS documents two with
different Health endpoints.

26
00:01:29.565 --> 00:01:33.440
For commercial accounts the
active endpoint is in U S East 1.

27
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.949
In AWS GovCloud there's a single
regional endpoint, in U S Gov West 1,

28
00:01:37.949 --> 00:01:43.126
and AWS's GovCloud guide says EventBridge
there doesn't send public events

29
00:01:43.126 --> 00:01:48.303
from the Service Health view, so a
scheduled read through the API is the path.

30
00:01:48.303 --> 00:01:49.814
The kit never guesses.

31
00:01:49.814 --> 00:01:53.180
The settings name the partition,
the region comes from it,

32
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.332
and the caller's identity must be in
that partition, because a GovCloud

33
00:01:57.332 --> 00:02:01.990
account read from the commercial
endpoint fails, or worse, reads nothing.

34
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.815
Here's AWS's starting point.

35
00:02:07.815 --> 00:02:13.710
The Health API reference defines the event:
its service, its type code and category,

36
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.964
its region, its times, its status,

37
00:02:15.964 --> 00:02:19.778
and newer fields such as
actionability and personas.

38
00:02:19.778 --> 00:02:22.390
The command line's describe
events lists them.

39
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.205
A second operation returns each
event's latest description,

40
00:02:26.205 --> 00:02:30.402
one to ten events at a time, and a
third names the affected resources.

41
00:02:30.402 --> 00:02:32.827
The API needs a qualifying support plan,

42
00:02:32.827 --> 00:02:35.840
or it answers with a
subscription-required error.

43
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.723
And AWS's policy examples grant a wildcard
over the describe actions; AWS publishes

44
00:02:41.723 --> 00:02:47.630
no read-only managed policy for Health,
and the full-access one includes writes.

45
00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:56.080
One point deserves its own slide,
because AWS's own pages don't agree.

46
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.943
The Health concepts page names
the newer Business Support Plus,

47
00:02:59.943 --> 00:03:02.567
Enterprise Support or Unified Operations.

48
00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.717
The API reference's welcome
page and the GovCloud

49
00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:08.943
page name the older Business
and Enterprise plans.

50
00:03:08.943 --> 00:03:11.968
Without a qualifying plan,
the API answers with a

51
00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:15.068
subscription-required
error, and nothing is read.

52
00:03:15.068 --> 00:03:18.621
So the chapter's advice is
simple: confirm the account's

53
00:03:18.621 --> 00:03:22.331
plan with AWS Support rather
than reading it off one page.

54
00:03:22.331 --> 00:03:27.153
The kit surfaces the error in AWS's
own words, the runbook's pre-flight

55
00:03:27.153 --> 00:03:32.070
calls the API first, and the Health
Dashboard is the interim path.

56
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.620
What does AWS leave to you?

57
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.519
The event list comes without descriptions
or resources, and an event without

58
00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:46.958
them can't be routed, so the kit fetches
both for every event, ten at a time.

59
00:03:46.958 --> 00:03:49.770
No category says retirement or security.

60
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.119
AWS's actionability flag is AWS's own,

61
00:03:53.119 --> 00:03:57.807
so the kit uses it as published,
and labels it as AWS's.

62
00:03:57.807 --> 00:04:00.821
A retirement without that
flag could look optional.

63
00:04:00.821 --> 00:04:02.370
Times are epoch seconds.

64
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.170
And a wildcard policy
grows as AWS adds actions.

65
00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.195
Each gap is pinned by a test.

66
00:04:08.195 --> 00:04:13.561
The verdict: use AWS's three operations
as published, with exactly three actions,

67
00:04:13.561 --> 00:04:17.730
and build the batching, the
kinds and the partition guard.

68
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.418
Now the code.

69
00:04:22.418 --> 00:04:24.868
The collector makes three declared calls.

70
00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:29.643
First, describe events, filtered to
events that are open or upcoming.

71
00:04:29.643 --> 00:04:34.371
Then, for those events, the details
call for each one's latest description,

72
00:04:34.371 --> 00:04:39.098
and the affected-entities call for
the resources it names, each ten event

73
00:04:39.098 --> 00:04:43.668
identifiers at a time, because AWS's
details operation takes one to ten.

74
00:04:43.668 --> 00:04:45.868
Then the normalizer builds the record.

75
00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:49.497
The title comes from the
service and the event type code,

76
00:04:49.497 --> 00:04:52.180
like E C 2, instance retirement scheduled.

77
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.468
A closed status closes the record.

78
00:04:54.468 --> 00:04:59.550
And in a live run, the region is the
partition's own Health endpoint region.

79
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.600
The normalizer maps AWS's
categories to the record's kinds.

80
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.100
Issues and investigations are incidents.

81
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.918
A scheduled change whose event type code
names a retirement, a lifecycle event,

82
00:05:14.918 --> 00:05:17.957
a deprecation or an end of
support is a retirement;

83
00:05:17.957 --> 00:05:20.625
any other scheduled change is maintenance.

84
00:05:20.625 --> 00:05:24.582
An account notification with the
security persona is security;

85
00:05:24.582 --> 00:05:26.225
otherwise it's an advisory.

86
00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:30.358
For every kind, AWS's
actionability flag decides first,

87
00:05:30.358 --> 00:05:32.563
and the record says provider.

88
00:05:32.563 --> 00:05:36.718
Only a retirement without that
flag falls to the kit's own rule:

89
00:05:36.718 --> 00:05:40.549
action required, due at its
start, and the record says rule.

90
00:05:40.549 --> 00:05:42.860
The two sources are never mixed.

91
00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.885
Before a live run reads anything,
the kit checks who it's signed in as.

92
00:05:50.885 --> 00:05:55.260
It asks AWS's security token service
for the caller's identity, once,

93
00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.272
and refuses a caller whose identity
isn't in the partition the settings name.

94
00:06:00.272 --> 00:06:03.198
The region is that partition's
Health endpoint region.

95
00:06:03.198 --> 00:06:07.917
When the tool fails, for example with
the subscription-required error,

96
00:06:07.917 --> 00:06:10.359
the error surfaces in AWS's own words.

97
00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:14.036
The tests pin all of this: six
in the collector's AWS block,

98
00:06:14.036 --> 00:06:17.393
plus the live tests for
the endpoint per partition,

99
00:06:17.393 --> 00:06:22.720
the eleven-identifier refusal, the
caller guard and the support-plan error.

100
00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.520
Who's involved?

101
00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.295
Developers maintain the kit.

102
00:06:29.295 --> 00:06:34.070
The AWS account owner owns this
gate, including the reader's policy.

103
00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:38.014
And whoever owns the support plan
confirms it with AWS Support,

104
00:06:38.014 --> 00:06:40.820
or records the gap with
the security officer.

105
00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.989
The policy is the whole permission
story: a customer managed policy

106
00:06:44.989 --> 00:06:49.382
with exactly the three describe actions
the kit calls, and nothing else.

107
00:06:49.382 --> 00:06:53.993
Not AWS's wildcard example, because
a wildcard grows as AWS adds actions,

108
00:06:53.993 --> 00:06:58.369
and never the full-access managed
policy attached for convenience,

109
00:06:58.369 --> 00:07:00.920
because then the reader can write.

110
00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:09.508
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7 with
Pester, working in the kit's folder.

111
00:07:09.508 --> 00:07:13.033
The lab settings name AWS's
own documentation account,

112
00:07:13.033 --> 00:07:16.245
in the commercial partition,
and nothing signs in.

113
00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:21.329
Step 1 lists what AWS needs: three
calls, each with one read action,

114
00:07:21.329 --> 00:07:25.070
and each verified against
AWS's documentation.

115
00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.457
That's the whole policy.

116
00:07:26.457 --> 00:07:32.018
Step 2 shows each partition's Health
endpoint region and the prefix a caller's

117
00:07:32.018 --> 00:07:37.420
identity must carry: U S East 1 for
commercial, U S Gov West 1 for GovCloud.

118
00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.782
Step 3 collects AWS from
the day-one fixtures.

119
00:07:44.782 --> 00:07:47.819
Five notices, four open,
three needing action,

120
00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:50.445
and every one valid against the schema.

121
00:07:50.445 --> 00:07:52.620
Step 4 reads them as a table.

122
00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.189
Two retirements, an instance retirement
and a Lambda lifecycle event,

123
00:07:57.189 --> 00:08:00.182
each with a due date and
the affected resource.

124
00:08:00.182 --> 00:08:01.994
Maintenance on a database.

125
00:08:01.994 --> 00:08:03.783
A security notification.

126
00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:05.183
And a closed incident.

127
00:08:05.183 --> 00:08:09.274
Notice that every row but the incident
names the resource it affects,

128
00:08:09.274 --> 00:08:13.360
because the kit fetched the
affected entities for every event.

129
00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:20.235
Step 5 shows who said
each notice needs action.

130
00:08:20.235 --> 00:08:23.130
The instance retirement and the security

131
00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.522
notification carry AWS's
own actionability flag.

132
00:08:26.522 --> 00:08:29.508
The Lambda lifecycle event
carries the kit's rule:

133
00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:34.023
a scheduled change whose type code names
a retirement, due at its start time.

134
00:08:34.023 --> 00:08:37.594
Step 6 switches the partition
to GovCloud in memory and

135
00:08:37.594 --> 00:08:41.322
builds the command a live run
would use, without sending it.

136
00:08:41.322 --> 00:08:43.847
The region is U S Gov West 1.

137
00:08:43.847 --> 00:08:47.923
Step 7 asks for details of
eleven events in one call.

138
00:08:47.923 --> 00:08:53.890
AWS's operation takes one to ten, and
the kit refuses before anything runs.

139
00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.540
Step 8 runs this chapter's tests:
eleven passed, none failed,

140
00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:05.440
and the manifest lists the
three calls with their actions.

141
00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:06.940
Then delete the output.

142
00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:12.878
Lab 2 moves to an AWS test account with a
qualifying support plan, never production.

143
00:09:12.878 --> 00:09:17.939
Without the plan, the subscription-required
error at step 3 is the expected result.

144
00:09:17.939 --> 00:09:23.078
Create the policy with exactly the three
actions and attach it to the reader's role.

145
00:09:23.078 --> 00:09:27.666
Check who you're signed in as: the identity
must be in the commercial partition.

146
00:09:27.666 --> 00:09:31.853
Cross-check AWS's own event count
against the kit's collection.

147
00:09:31.853 --> 00:09:35.106
Then detach and delete the
policy, and delete the run,

148
00:09:35.106 --> 00:09:38.040
because it names the account's resources.

149
00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.590
Each output is evidence for something
specific, against NIST's control catalog.

150
00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:50.907
The three-action policy, and the reads
going to the partition's own endpoint

151
00:09:50.907 --> 00:09:55.502
as a caller in that partition, support
monitoring an external service provider.

152
00:09:55.502 --> 00:10:00.481
The run folder shows every open and upcoming
event was read with its description

153
00:10:00.481 --> 00:10:05.014
and resources, which supports receiving
security alerts and advisories.

154
00:10:05.014 --> 00:10:09.884
And events AWS marks action required
carry AWS's due date, which supports

155
00:10:09.884 --> 00:10:15.240
carrying out directives in their time
frames, through the task Chapter 6 routes.

156
00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.215
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
and the mapping is the program's.

157
00:10:19.215 --> 00:10:23.660
The kit's records support these
controls; the assessor decides them.

158
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.285
What breaks in production,
and what guards against it?

159
00:10:30.285 --> 00:10:35.054
No support plan, and nothing is read:
the error surfaces, the runbook's

160
00:10:35.054 --> 00:10:40.335
pre-flight calls the API first, and the
Health Dashboard is the interim path.

161
00:10:40.335 --> 00:10:44.689
The full-access policy attached for
convenience lets the reader write,

162
00:10:44.689 --> 00:10:46.460
so the kit uses three actions.

163
00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.680
A GovCloud account read from
the commercial endpoint fails,

164
00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.835
so the partition names the region
and the caller is checked.

165
00:10:52.835 --> 00:10:56.660
Eleven identifiers in one call
are refused before calling.

166
00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:00.917
A retirement without AWS's flag
could stop an instance on its date,

167
00:11:00.917 --> 00:11:03.598
so the kit's rule makes
it action required.

168
00:11:03.598 --> 00:11:07.192
And in GovCloud, EventBridge
won't bring public events,

169
00:11:07.192 --> 00:11:09.470
so the kit reads on a schedule.

170
00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:14.570
Here's what done looks like.

171
00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.212
The account has a qualifying support plan,

172
00:11:17.212 --> 00:11:20.232
or the gap is recorded
with the security officer.

173
00:11:20.232 --> 00:11:24.745
The reader's policy holds exactly the
three describe actions the kit calls.

174
00:11:24.745 --> 00:11:28.758
The settings name the partition,
and the caller's identity is in it.

175
00:11:28.758 --> 00:11:32.946
AWS's own count and the kit's
collection agree in the test account.

176
00:11:32.946 --> 00:11:36.170
Every retirement has a
due date and its resource.

177
00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.157
And the test policy was
detached and deleted.

178
00:11:39.157 --> 00:11:43.255
Next, Chapter 5: Google Cloud's
Personalized Service Health,

179
00:11:43.255 --> 00:11:46.433
the smallest collector,
with the largest gaps.

180
00:11:46.433 --> 00:11:47.590
Thanks for watching.
